    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q13a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that if $n^2$ is divisible by $3$, then $n$ is divisible by $3$. Note: You will need to consider three cases: $n=3k,\\,3k+1,\\,3k+2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The squares of integers of the forms $3k$, $3k+1$ and $3k+2$ have remainders $0$, $1$ and $1$ respectively when divided by $3$. Thus $3\\mid n^2$ requires $n=3k$, so $3\\mid n$.",
          "worked": "Every integer has one of the forms $3k$, $3k+1$ or $3k+2$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nIf $n=3k$,\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2&=9k^2\\\\n^2&=3(3k^2).\\end{align*}$$\nIf $n=3k+1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2&=9k^2+6k+1\\\\n^2&=3(3k^2+2k)+1.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $n=3k+2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2&=9k^2+12k+4\\\\n^2&=3(3k^2+4k+1)+1.\\end{align*}$$\nOnly the first case gives a square divisible by $3$. Therefore, if $n^2$ is divisible by $3$, then $n=3k$ and $n$ is divisible by $3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q13b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that $\\sqrt3$ is irrational.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt3$ is irrational.\n\nA fraction in lowest terms would force both its numerator and denominator to be divisible by $3$, contradicting lowest terms.",
          "worked": "Suppose $\\sqrt3$ is rational. Write $\\sqrt3=\\frac{m}{n}$ in lowest terms, where $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $n\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{m^2}{n^2}&=3\\\\m^2&=3n^2.\\end{align*}$$\nBy part (a), $3\\mid m$. Put $m=3k$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(3k)^2&=3n^2\\\\9k^2&=3n^2\\\\n^2&=3k^2.\\end{align*}$$\nAgain by part (a), $3\\mid n$. Thus $m$ and $n$ have the common factor $3$, contradicting that the fraction is in lowest terms. Therefore $\\sqrt3$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
  },
